## Mathematical statement and scope

For eta > 0, L > 0, and finite nuclear data with nonnegative charges, the
formalized periodic Born--Oppenheimer Hamiltonian has a normalized ground pair
on the electronic space. On every sufficiently large ordinary Fourier cube,

  0 <= EN-E <= C_cutoff/K^3,   EN-E <= C_basis/N,
  K = (2*pi/L)*m,             N = (2*m+1)^3.

Here E is the lowest eigenvalue of H on Λ^η(L²(T³) ⊗ ℂ²), and EN is the
lowest eigenvalue of PHP on ran P ∩ Λ^η(L²(T³) ⊗ ℂ²), where P projects every
spin component onto the cube. Every state of ran P lies in the H2 domain of H
(IsMolecularSpinCubic.exists_hamiltonian), so the minimum runs over all of
ran P ∩ Λ^η(L²(T³) ⊗ ℂ²). N counts one-particle plane waves, not
many-particle configurations. Cubic energies are attained Rayleigh minima; a
cubic minimizer need not be an eigenvector of the full continuum Hamiltonian.

Electronic states are spin-dependent wavefunctions with one L2 component for
each spin configuration σ : Fin eta → Fin 2, antisymmetric under simultaneous
exchange of the positions and spins of two electrons (MolecularSpinL2,
IsSpinFermionic). The spin-independent Hamiltonian acts on each component.
The σ-component of such a state lies in the exchange sector of σ (the
labelling spinSectorLabel σ of the Born--Oppenheimer system), that is, it is
antisymmetric under exchange of electrons with equal spins.
The sector results hold for every labelling. The constant labelling is the
fully antisymmetric spatial sector, which describes the fully spin-polarised
states. The tail reduction is applied to each particle separately, so the
proof uses no exchange symmetry beyond membership in the sector, and the
constants do not depend on the sector.

The Hamiltonian is independently specified by weak kinetic differentiation
and actual signed Coulomb multiplication, with its full H2 graph identified.
Coordinates are dimensionless x=r/L on the unit torus; the kinetic scaling
is -(1/(2*L^2))*Delta. Nuclear attraction uses x_i-R_A and electron pairs are
counted once. The periodic Coulomb zero mode is removed. Nuclear repulsion
is an absorbed constant energy shift.

The periodic remainder, Hardy and tail bounds, required H2 estimates,
ground-pair existence, and finite/continuum Ritz identification are proved.
No external potential, decomposition, symmetry, or spectral hypothesis is
an input to the main error theorem. The data-only error budget uses the
operator norm of one specified finite reference Hamiltonian, on the cube of
radius eta. molecularFiniteReferenceEnergyBound_le_explicit bounds that norm
by (3/2)(2*pi/L)^2*eta^3 + B*sqrt(1 + 3*(2*pi/L)^2*eta^3), where B is the
molecular H1 form constant; molecularGroundEnergyBudget_le_explicit bounds
the energy budget accordingly. The constants and thresholds are explicit and
conservative. Milestone 151 bounds the cutoff constants and the cube-radius
threshold by polynomials in eta and Z_tot whose coefficients depend only on L:
with Q = 1 + eta + Z_tot,
  C_cutoff(E) <= 4 F_L eta^2 Q^14 (1+|E|)^3,
  C_cutoff <= 32 F_L (1+G_L)^3 eta^2 Q^26 (1+eta^3)^3,
  molecularDataCubicThreshold <= T_L (1+eta) (1+eta^3)^2 Q^20
for the energy-dependent constant, the data-only constant and the data-only
cube-radius threshold (molecularCanonicalCubicErrorConstant_le_coarse,
molecularDataCubicErrorConstant_le_coarse, molecularDataCubicThreshold_le_poly).
Here F_L = coulombPolyCellFactor L, G_L = coulombBudgetCellFactor L and
T_L = coulombThresholdCellFactor L. This is the polynomial dependence on eta
and λ_ζ = Z_tot that thm:main states. The basis-count constants are
(3L/(2*pi))^3 times the cutoff constants, by definition, and the radii of the
Accuracy statements are the larger of the threshold and ⌈C_basis/epsilon⌉, so
they are bounded in the same way (with 1/epsilon). The Convergence statements
only assert that a starting radius m0 exists. The dependence on L is not
polynomial.

For a Coulomb system the Hamiltonian is
  H = sum_a |k_a|^2/(2 m_a) + sum_{a<b} q_a q_b w(x_a-x_b)
      + sum_a sum_l q_a Z_l w(x_a-R_l),
again independently specified, by the mass-weighted weak kinetic equation and
charge-weighted Coulomb multiplication (HasCoulombHamiltonian). With masses
m_a >= 1, mbar = max_a m_a and S_r = sum_a m_a^r, the same bounds hold with
C_cutoff = 64 C1 S_1 S_2 C_W^2 (the manuscript's mbar^2 S_1 S_2 Theta^2 form):
C1 = 1/2 + B + |E| with the mass-free form constant B, and the tail constant
C_W carries the a priori H2 bound mbar (2 + 8(|E|+1) + 16 B_m^2). The H1
constant B_m weights the pair (a, b) by sqrt(min(m_a, m_b)) and the
attraction of particle a to the static nuclei by sqrt(m_a), so only pairs of
two heavy particles and the attraction of a heavy particle to the static
nuclei cost a mass factor.
The cutoff conditions are 4 mbar (|E|+1) <= K^2 and
32 S_2 C_W^2 <= K^5, and |E| <= U + B_m^2 + 1/4. The non-BO problem is the
case without static nuclei. With spin, the state space is
L²((T³)^η; ⊗_a ℂ^{d_a}), written in components for the spin configurations
σ : Fin eta → Fin D; its ground energy is the lowest ground energy of the
exchange sectors of the valid spin patterns, so the same bounds hold with the
same constants. Every state of ran P also lies in the domain of
the Hamiltonian of every Coulomb system
(IsMolecularPositionCubic.exists_coulombHamiltonian), so the cubic minima of a
Coulomb system also run over all of ran P in the sector. With the charge
weight Q = 1 + sum_a |q_a| + Z_tot (CoulombSystem.chargeWeight), the constants
of a Coulomb system satisfy C_cutoff(E) <= 4 F_L eta^2 mbar^7 Q^14 (1+|E|)^3,
the coarse form eta_tot^2 mbar^7 (1+|E|)^3 of thm:nbo (eta_tot is the number
eta of quantum particles), and the data-only constant and cube radius are
bounded by polynomials in eta, mbar and Q (milestone 151).

This release covers the periodic ground-energy convergence theorem for the BO
problem and for Coulomb systems, with spin and in every sector of an
admissible labelling. The grid files PlaneWave.GridCore.lean and
PlaneWave.Grid.lean formalise the grid theorem thm:grid-energy
for Coulomb systems, on the spin-inclusive space and in the sector of every
admissible labelling, and its Born--Oppenheimer case, for every kernel of the
manuscript's kernel class K_K with real, even coefficients (the sharp truncated
kernel among them); they bound the grid constant and threshold by polynomials
in eta, mbar and Q. Part II and the grid release file cover the dynamical and
kinetic-energy results and prop:fullkernel (see Part II below); the
free-boundary systems and the appendices on the projection flux and on the
kinetic-energy source are not covered. It does not claim the manuscript's
precise constants are reproduced.

